cff-version: 1.2.0
message: "If you use this work, please cite it using the metadata below. Do not cite the figures of version 1.0.0 (1.84% to 8.43%, 190.3 ha): they are withdrawn."
title: "Stolen Strata: Brick Kilns and the Loss of Karewa Tableland in Budgam, Kashmir, 1993-2025, with a Correction to an Earlier Estimate"
type: software
authors:
  - family-names: "Maske"
    given-names: "Sakshi D."
repository-code: "https://github.com/sakshimaske303-commits/STOLEN_STRATA"
url: "https://github.com/sakshimaske303-commits/STOLEN_STRATA"
identifiers:
  - type: doi
    value: "10.5281/zenodo.21766464"
    description: "Zenodo archive of version 1.0.0, the earlier version whose main results are withdrawn. Version 2 is not yet archived."
abstract: >-
  A satellite study of vegetated karewa tableland lost to brick kilns in the
  Kashmir Valley. An earlier version reported that the bare-earth share of
  karewa terraces rose from 1.84% in 1994 to 8.43% in 2025; that result came
  from comparing Landsat composites with a differently built Sentinel-2
  composite and is withdrawn. This version maps 180 scarp-bounded karewa
  terraces (173.3 square kilometres) from the Copernicus elevation model using
  slope, height above drainage and scarp share, checks the map against a
  published geological map, and follows the yearly peak NDVI of every 30 m
  pixel using Landsat only. Between 2013-2015 and 2023-2025 a strict test
  finds 107 ha of terrace land converted from vegetated to bare and a looser
  test 329 ha. A hand-labelled sample of 120 points indicates that about
  141 ha of the flagged land (95% interval roughly 110-172 ha) lies inside
  brick-kiln fields, concentrated in two belts in Budgam district, Rangeen
  Kultreh and Bandagam-Batapora. Depth of excavation could not be measured,
  and no link to the saffron tablelands of Pampore was found.
keywords:
  - karewa
  - Kashmir Valley
  - brick kilns
  - land conversion
  - Landsat time series
  - sensor consistency
  - accuracy assessment
  - Google Earth Engine
